    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is clean and matches the established pattern in this package.\n\n- **Right unit / responsibility.** Producing a *unique* `customerCode` is a natural part of \"assign the customer code,\" so keeping the de-dup loop inside `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` is coherent rather than catch-all. The class still does one thing: derive and set the code.\n- **Idiomatic, follows a sibling.** `CountOwnerNamesakes` is the direct precedent \u2014 same `service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)` signature, same \"scan `findAll()` to derive a create-time attribute, then set a field on the owner\" shape. Injecting the repository into the function and iterating `findAll()` is the house style here, not an anomaly.\n- **No duplication introduced.** There's no existing \"set of existing customer codes\" helper to reuse, and the de-dup logic isn't a reusable domain concept the way `OwnerRegion` / `HouseholdKey` / `OwnerIdentity` are, so there's no reason to extract a value object for it. Inlining is the right call.\n- **Javadoc updated** to match the new behavior, consistent with how the sibling classes document their derivation rules.\n\nTrivial, optional nit (not blocking): the new `import java.util.HashSet; import java.util.Set;` are placed in their own block separated by a blank line from the other `java.*` imports, whereas the convention elsewhere (e.g. `HouseholdKey`) keeps all `java.*` imports in one group. Worth folding into the existing java import group if you touch the file again, but not worth a round-trip on its own.",
